## 4. Competing Approaches

### C

C provides `enum` (a weak alias for `int`) and `union` (untagged). To build a discriminated union, a programmer must manually combine them and switch on the tag. Nothing prevents writing to the wrong union member:

```c
enum Tag { INT, FLOAT };
struct Value {
    enum Tag tag;
    union {
        int i;
        float f;
    } data;
};
// Accidentally read data.f when tag == INT → UB
```

Zig’s exhaustive switch and language‑level `union(enum)` eliminate this entire class of bugs at compile time.

### C++

`std::variant` is the standard tagged union. It uses templates and `std::visit` for access:

```cpp
std::variant<int, float, std::string> v = 3.14f;
std::visit([](auto&& arg) { std::cout << arg; }, v);
```

The visitor is a hidden control‑flow mechanism—the library dispatches based on the index. Exceptions may be thrown on invalid access. Memory is stack‑allocated (no heap), but the implementation can be heavy at compile time due to template recursion. C++ also offers OOP hierarchies with virtual functions, which entangle data and code, require heap allocation, and bring RTTI overhead. Zig’s approach keeps the tag and data local, and the dispatch is a plain `switch`—no templates, no exceptions, no hidden vtable lookups.

### Rust

Rust’s `enum` with data is structurally similar:

```rust
enum Payload {
    Int(i64),
    Float(f64),
    Text(String),
    None,
}
let p = Payload::Int(42);
match p {
    Payload::Int(val) => println!("int {}", val),
    // exhaustive match required.
}
```

Rust’s enums are stack‑allocated and pattern matching is exhaustive. The key difference is that Rust’s ownership and borrowing model interacts with variants—moving or borrowing a payload can be subtle when the enum is behind a reference. Zig has no borrow checker; you simply take a pointer to the payload after the `switch` capture, with all the manual responsibility that entails. This grants maximum control at the cost of safety. Rust’s `Option` and `Result` are also enums, whereas Zig uses nullable pointers and error unions as separate primitives, which can result in less nesting in some patterns.

### Go

Go has no sum types. The common workaround is an `interface{}` with a type switch:

```go
var v interface{} = 42
switch val := v.(type) {
case int:
    fmt.Println("int", val)
case string:
    fmt.Println("string", val)
}
```

This boxes every value on the heap (for non‑pointer types) and requires runtime type reflection. There is no exhaustiveness check—forgetting a case silently falls through. Zig’s tagged unions are a compile‑time concept with zero allocation overhead.

### Python

Python relies on `isinstance` and ad‑hoc unions or the `typing.Union` hint. There is no runtime enforcement. Zig’s static checks prevent an entire category of “attribute not found” errors before the program ever runs.

